{
  "slug": "artist:tony-allen",
  "name": "Tony Allen",
  "editorial_article": "Born in Lagos on 20 July 1940, Tony Oladipo Allen became the foundational architect of the Afrobeat rhythm as the drummer and musical director for Fela Kuti's band, Africa '70, between 1968 and 1979. Fela famously claimed the genre could not exist without Allen, whose unique playing style blended Yoruba percussion, highlife, and jazz influences from artists like Art Blakey. After leaving Africa '70 in 1979, Allen moved to London and later Paris, where his work evolved to incorporate electronic, hip-hop, and dub elements. He later maintained a prolific collaborative career, partnering with Damon Albarn on projects such as The Good, the Bad & the Queen and Rocket Juice & the Moon. His 2006 album 'Lagos No Shaking' marked a return to traditional Afrobeat, while his final years included work with Jeff Mills and posthumous releases like 'There Is No End'.",
